§§§ `cl.4's two exclusion limbs, one assertion per named merchant and per kind`

-- THE DESCRIPTION LIMB. One line per merchant cl.4 names, in the bullets'
-- order, each as the `Description pattern` layer 1 assigns to that merchant's
-- statement line. These are unit tests on the predicate rather than on the
-- exported rule, because a statement line carries a LIST and the interesting
-- cases are the lists, not the transactions around them.
#ASSERT `cl.4 — the description names a transaction the clause excludes` (LIST `Forex or CFD platform`)      -- bullet 1  Forex.com
#ASSERT `cl.4 — the description names a transaction the clause excludes` (LIST `Cryptocurrency platform`)    -- bullet 4  crypto-currencies of any kind
#ASSERT `cl.4 — the description names a transaction the clause excludes` (LIST PayPal)                       -- bullet 5  Paypal
#ASSERT `cl.4 — the description names a transaction the clause excludes` (LIST `Skrill or Moneybookers`)     -- bullet 5  SKR skrill.com
#ASSERT `cl.4 — the description names a transaction the clause excludes` (LIST CardUp)                       -- bullet 5  CardUp
#ASSERT `cl.4 — the description names a transaction the clause excludes` (LIST SmoovPay)                     -- bullet 5  SmoovPay
#ASSERT `cl.4 — the description names a transaction the clause excludes` (LIST iPaymy)                       -- bullet 5  iPayMy
#ASSERT `cl.4 — the description names a transaction the clause excludes` (LIST `EZ-Link`)                    -- bullet 7  EZ-Link
#ASSERT `cl.4 — the description names a transaction the clause excludes` (LIST TRANSIT)                      -- bullet 7  Transitlink
#ASSERT `cl.4 — the description names a transaction the clause excludes` (LIST FlashPay)                     -- bullet 7  NETS Flashpay
#ASSERT `cl.4 — the description names a transaction the clause excludes` (LIST YouTrip)                      -- bullet 7  Youtrip
#ASSERT `cl.4 — the description names a transaction the clause excludes` (LIST AXS)                          -- bullet 9  "Any AXS and ATM transactions"

-- A statement line carries every family it matches, and one match is enough.
-- "AMAZE*AXS PTE LTD" carries both; AMAZE alone is not a cl.4 exclusion on
-- this card, AXS is, and the line is excluded.
#ASSERT `cl.4 — the description names a transaction the clause excludes` (LIST AMAZE, AXS)
#ASSERT NOT (`cl.4 — the description names a transaction the clause excludes` (LIST AMAZE))

-- The negative control, which is what makes the twelve lines above mean
-- anything: families the clause does NOT name, and a line that carries none.
#ASSERT NOT (`cl.4 — the description names a transaction the clause excludes` (LIST FavePay))
#ASSERT NOT (`cl.4 — the description names a transaction the clause excludes` (LIST ShopeePay, `BUS/MRT`, `Government levy`))
#ASSERT NOT (`cl.4 — the description names a transaction the clause excludes` (`a wallet tap at a restaurant`'s `description patterns`))

-- THE CHARGE-KIND LIMB, bullets 3, 9, 15, 16, 17 and 18.
#ASSERT `cl.4 — the kind of charge is one the clause excludes` `Balance or funds transfer`     -- bullet 15  balance transfers, fund transfers
#ASSERT `cl.4 — the kind of charge is one the clause excludes` `Cash advance or quasi-cash`    -- bullets 3, 9, 15  quasi-cash, ATM, cash advances
#ASSERT `cl.4 — the kind of charge is one the clause excludes` `Interest or finance charge`    -- bullet 15  finance charges, late charges
#ASSERT `cl.4 — the kind of charge is one the clause excludes` `Annual fee or bank charge`     -- bullet 15  "any fees charged by HSBC"
#ASSERT `cl.4 — the kind of charge is one the clause excludes` `Instalment plan transaction`   -- bullets 15, 16, 17
#ASSERT `cl.4 — the kind of charge is one the clause excludes` `Refund or reversal`            -- bullet 18  refunded and cancelled

-- And the four kinds this document does NOT exclude. The last three are the
-- encoding decisions recorded at the rule: a bill payment and a mail order are
-- excluded by name in other issuers' lists and not in HSBC's, and a
-- pre-authorisation is excluded only on a reading of the chapeau that the
-- bullets do not support. If any of these is ever read into cl.4, one of these
-- four assertions is where it has to be argued for.
#ASSERT NOT (`cl.4 — the kind of charge is one the clause excludes` `Retail purchase`)
#ASSERT NOT (`cl.4 — the kind of charge is one the clause excludes` `Bill payment via internet banking`)
#ASSERT NOT (`cl.4 — the kind of charge is one the clause excludes` `Telephone or mail order`)
#ASSERT NOT (`cl.4 — the kind of charge is one the clause excludes` `Pre-authorisation`)


§§§ `The two new limbs, end to end`

-- Each fixture below is a transaction that would earn the bonus rate but for
-- ONE field. Holding everything else at a known 10-point case is the point:
-- if the answer moves, the field that moved it is the only candidate.

-- bullets 16 and 17. The same restaurant tap as the first illustration, posted
-- as a monthly instalment: "The monthly instalment amounts under all card
-- instalment plan (including HSBC 0% Card Instalment Payment Plan, HSBC
-- PayLater Instalment Plan and HSBC Spend Instalment)".
GIVETH A Transaction
`an instalment on a restaurant purchase` MEANS Transaction WITH
    `merchant name`           IS "Tiong Bahru Bakery"
    `merchant`                IS `Another merchant`
    `merchant category code`  IS 5812
    `transaction description` IS "HSBC SPEND INSTALMENT 3 OF 12"
    `description patterns`    IS EMPTY
    `charge kind`             IS `Instalment plan transaction`
    `amount`                  IS 38.50
    `currency`                IS "SGD"
    `posted in a foreign currency` IS FALSE
    `transaction date`        IS YMD 2026 5 4
    `posting date`            IS YMD 2026 5 6
    `payment channel`         IS `Tapped at a terminal`
    `credential`              IS `A mobile wallet token`
    `merchant indicator`      IS `Flags card-present`
    `made in Singapore`       IS TRUE

-- bullet 18, the refunded limb. Same tap, reversed.
GIVETH A Transaction
`a refund of a restaurant tap` MEANS Transaction WITH
    `merchant name`           IS "Tiong Bahru Bakery"
    `merchant`                IS `Another merchant`
    `merchant category code`  IS 5812
    `transaction description` IS "TIONG BAHRU BAKERY SINGAPORE SG REVERSAL"
    `description patterns`    IS EMPTY
    `charge kind`             IS `Refund or reversal`
    `amount`                  IS 38.50
    `currency`                IS "SGD"
    `posted in a foreign currency` IS FALSE
    `transaction date`        IS YMD 2026 5 4
    `posting date`            IS YMD 2026 5 8
    `payment channel`         IS `Tapped at a terminal`
    `credential`              IS `A mobile wallet token`
    `merchant indicator`      IS `Flags card-present`
    `made in Singapore`       IS TRUE

-- bullet 15, the fee limb: "any fees charged by HSBC". An annual fee posts to
-- the Account rather than at a merchant, so the MCC and the channel below are
-- the ones that would otherwise pay -- which is exactly the point of holding
-- them fixed.
GIVETH A Transaction
`the annual fee charged to the Account` MEANS Transaction WITH
    `merchant name`           IS "HSBC"
    `merchant`                IS `Another merchant`
    `merchant category code`  IS 5812
    `transaction description` IS "HSBC ANNUAL FEE"
    `description patterns`    IS EMPTY
    `charge kind`             IS `Annual fee or bank charge`
    `amount`                  IS 160
    `currency`                IS "SGD"
    `posted in a foreign currency` IS FALSE
    `transaction date`        IS YMD 2026 5 4
    `posting date`            IS YMD 2026 5 6
    `payment channel`         IS `Tapped at a terminal`
    `credential`              IS `A mobile wallet token`
    `merchant indicator`      IS `Flags card-present`
    `made in Singapore`       IS TRUE

-- bullet 7, the description limb, at an MCC that IS in the cl.7 table: MCC 5999
-- is row 42, and an online purchase the merchant flags online would otherwise
-- be an Eligible Transaction at 10 points. The statement line says YOUTRIP, so
-- cl.4 takes it out before cl.7 is reached.
GIVETH A Transaction
`a Youtrip top-up bought online` MEANS Transaction WITH
    `merchant name`           IS "YouTrip"
    `merchant`                IS `Another merchant`
    `merchant category code`  IS 5999
    `transaction description` IS "YOUTRIP SINGAPORE SG"
    `description patterns`    IS LIST YouTrip
    `charge kind`             IS `Retail purchase`
    `amount`                  IS 200
    `currency`                IS "SGD"
    `posted in a foreign currency` IS FALSE
    `transaction date`        IS YMD 2026 5 4
    `posting date`            IS YMD 2026 5 6
    `payment channel`         IS `Paid in a website or app`
    `credential`              IS `The physical card`
    `merchant indicator`      IS `Flags online`
    `made in Singapore`       IS TRUE

-- The control, restated here rather than left 500 lines up in the cl.5 block:
-- the restaurant tap pays 10, and the three charge-kind fixtures above differ
-- from it in exactly one field. The first line below is deliberately the same
-- assertion as the one in that block, so this differential can be read on its
-- own.
#ASSERT (`HSBC Revolution earns on` `HSBC Revolution` `a Cardholder with no Everyday Global Account deposits` `a month position this card does not read` `a wallet tap at a restaurant`)'s `points per dollar` EQUALS 10
#ASSERT (`HSBC Revolution earns on` `HSBC Revolution` `a Cardholder with no Everyday Global Account deposits` `a month position this card does not read` `an instalment on a restaurant purchase`)'s `points per dollar` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT (`HSBC Revolution earns on` `HSBC Revolution` `a Cardholder with no Everyday Global Account deposits` `a month position this card does not read` `a refund of a restaurant tap`)'s `points per dollar` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT (`HSBC Revolution earns on` `HSBC Revolution` `a Cardholder with no Everyday Global Account deposits` `a month position this card does not read` `the annual fee charged to the Account`)'s `points per dollar` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT (`HSBC Revolution earns on` `HSBC Revolution` `a Cardholder with no Everyday Global Account deposits` `a month position this card does not read` `a Youtrip top-up bought online`)'s `points per dollar` EQUALS 0

-- An excluded transaction is excluded at the higher Deposits ADB too: cl.4
-- defeats cl.7 whatever the cardholder's balance.
#ASSERT (`HSBC Revolution earns on` `HSBC Revolution` `a Cardholder at the SGD50,000 Deposits ADB threshold` `a month position this card does not read` `an instalment on a restaurant purchase`)'s `points per dollar` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT (`HSBC Revolution earns on` `HSBC Revolution` `a Cardholder at the SGD50,000 Deposits ADB threshold` `a month position this card does not read` `a Youtrip top-up bought online`)'s `miles per dollar` EQUALS 0

-- A zero answer is a Verified zero, not a hedge: cl.4 says so in terms.
#ASSERT (`HSBC Revolution earns on` `HSBC Revolution` `a Cardholder with no Everyday Global Account deposits` `a month position this card does not read` `a refund of a restaurant tap`)'s `status` EQUALS Verified
#ASSERT NOT ((`HSBC Revolution earns on` `HSBC Revolution` `a Cardholder with no Everyday Global Account deposits` `a month position this card does not read` `a Youtrip top-up bought online`)'s `depends on the merchant indicator`)

-- The cap on an excluded transaction is the uncapped one, because there are no
-- Bonus Points to cap. `pool` is set on both Caps and is deliberately NOT
-- asserted anywhere: it is a STRING, and an assertion that compared it would
-- be the one string comparison left in this module.
#ASSERT NOT ((`HSBC Revolution earns on` `HSBC Revolution` `a Cardholder with no Everyday Global Account deposits` `a month position this card does not read` `a refund of a restaurant tap`)'s `cap`)'s `capped`
#ASSERT ((`HSBC Revolution earns on` `HSBC Revolution` `a Cardholder at the SGD50,000 Deposits ADB threshold` `a month position this card does not read` `a wallet tap at a restaurant`)'s `cap`)'s `capped`
#ASSERT ((`HSBC Revolution earns on` `HSBC Revolution` `a Cardholder at the SGD50,000 Deposits ADB threshold` `a month position this card does not read` `a wallet tap at a restaurant`)'s `cap`)'s `bonus spend per month` EQUALS 1_200


-- Two evaluations of the exported rule, printing the whole `Rate answer` the
-- composer receives rather than one field of it. The first is the partial
-- answer, which is the shape worth looking at; the second is the top of the
-- card.
--
-- Both become Catala test scopes and `clerk test` runs them, but WITHOUT AN
-- EXPECTED OUTPUT: a `Rate answer` has no Catala JSON rendering, so the
-- exporter has nothing to record against. They prove the lowering executes end
-- to end; the assertions above are what check the answers.
#EVAL `HSBC Revolution earns on` `HSBC Revolution` `a Cardholder with no Everyday Global Account deposits` `a month position this card does not read` `an online purchase whose indicator is unknown`
#EVAL `HSBC Revolution earns on` `HSBC Revolution` `a Cardholder at the SGD50,000 Deposits ADB threshold` `a month position this card does not read` `a wallet tap at a restaurant`


-- ===========================================================================
-- DEFEASIBILITY SITES (spec section 4.5). Where a native DESPITE / SUBJECT TO /
-- exception construct would have collapsed several guards into one rule.
-- One entry per site: the clause, the shape in the text (general rule +
-- exception list), the L4 shape used instead, and what a native construct
-- would have bought.
-- ===========================================================================
--
-- SITE 1 -- cl.4, the eighteen-bullet exclusion list.
--   TEXT   A general rule with an exception list attached by one word:
--          "'Qualifying Transactions' refers to posted retail purchases …, and
--          shall exclude the following transactions: •…•…" (18 bullets).
--   L4     Not one rule but four: the general limb is never stated at all
--          (nothing in this module says "a posted retail purchase qualifies"),
--          and the exclusions are three separate predicates -- one over the
--          statement line's `Description pattern`s, one over the posting's
--          `Charge kind`, one over the MCC -- filling the first three arms of
--          the top-level BRANCH so they defeat everything below them.
--   BOUGHT A native construct would let the general limb BE WRITTEN, which is
--          the part that has gone missing here. As it stands the positive rule
--          exists only as the absence of a match, so a reader cannot check the
--          encoding against the chapeau -- there is no sentence to compare it
--          to. That is the most expensive thing on this list, and the
--          `Charge kind` fact makes it sharper rather than softer: the chapeau
--          admits "POSTED retail purchases", and `Retail purchase` versus
--          `Pre-authorisation` is now a fact the module can see and still does
--          not act on, because no bullet tells it to.
--
-- SITE 2 -- cl.4 bullet 10, "Tax payments (except HSBC Tax Payment Facility)".
--   TEXT   An exception to an exception, in one parenthesis. MCC 9311 is
--          excluded by cl.4.2 row 44; the HSBC facility is carved back in.
--   L4     NOTHING. This is now a defeasibility site with no rule at it. The
--          carve-back used to be `AND NOT (… made through the HSBC Tax Payment
--          Facility)` bolted onto the cl.4.2 arm, testing the statement string;
--          moving off strings took the test away and the shared domain has no
--          constructor to put in its place. The two fixtures that used to
--          differ now answer identically, and an assertion pins that.
--   BOUGHT Nothing would be bought by a defeasibility construct here, which is
--          what makes this entry worth keeping: the obstacle is not the shape
--          of the rule but the poverty of the ontology. A construct that let
--          the carve-back out-rank the exclusion would still have nothing to
--          say WHICH transactions it applies to. This is the site that says
--          the expressive gap is sometimes in the facts, not the logic.
--
-- SITE 3 -- cl.4.2 laid over cl.4.
--   TEXT   Two different operations with the same effect. cl.4 says certain
--          transactions are NOT QUALIFYING TRANSACTIONS; cl.4.2 says
--          transactions at certain MCCs "WILL NOT EARN REWARD POINTS". The
--          second is a zero-earning rule over the top of the first, not a
--          carve-out from it.
--   L4     Separate arms of the top-level BRANCH -- two for cl.4, one for
--          cl.4.2 -- all returning the SAME zero answer, because on THIS
--          document the two lists are disjoint and the distinction cannot
--          change an outcome. The arms are separate; the answer is not.
--   BOUGHT The distinction survives. If a future revision put an MCC on both
--          lists, the collapsed encoding would answer zero and say nothing;
--          a construct with explicit priority would surface the overlap. The
--          disjointness is asserted in the illustrations so that the day it
--          stops being true, something fails.
--
-- SITE 4 -- cl.8 as the residual of cl.7.
--   TEXT   "1 Reward point for every SGD1 charged on ALL OTHER Qualifying
--          Transactions THAT ARE NOT ELIGIBLE TRANSACTIONS" -- a default
--          defined by subtraction from the rule above it.
--   L4     The `OTHERWISE` arm of the BRANCH. This is the one site where the
--          flat-cascade shape is a genuinely good fit.
--   BOUGHT Very little. Recorded because it is the base case that makes the
--          other sites legible as departures from it.
--
-- SITE 5 -- cl.7's heading against cl.7's table.
--   TEXT   The category heading reads "Dining EXCLUDING HOTEL DINING", and the
--          table three rows above it admits 7011 (Lodging), under which hotel
--          dining posts. The heading carves out what the table lets in.
--   L4     The table wins and the heading is encoded NOWHERE -- it survives
--          only as a comment -- because the clause's operative words are "that
--          fall within any one of the following MCCs".
--   BOUGHT This is the site that most wants Catala's conflict behaviour. A
--          native defeater would let the heading be ENCODED as a rule, and the
--          two would then be visibly incomparable on a hotel restaurant charge
--          -- which is the honest answer. Encoded by hand, the encoder's
--          judgement is silently baked in and the reader never learns there
--          was a question.
--
-- SITE 6 -- cl.2, the Programme period.
--   TEXT   Not an exception to a rule but a condition on the whole
--          instrument: "The Programme period starts from 1 April 2026".
--   L4     A wrapper rule that recomputes the answer and then RE-LISTS ALL
--          TWELVE FIELDS of the Rate answer to change two of them, because L4
--          has no record-update form.
--   BOUGHT A module-level SUBJECT TO would remove the twelve-field copy
--          entirely. The copy is the kind of code that goes wrong when the
--          record gains a thirteenth field, and nothing would warn.
--
-- SITE 7 -- cl.5's inclusive list against a credential it does not name.
--   TEXT   "a contactless terminal mode WHICH INCLUDES Visa payWave, Apple
--          Pay, Google Pay and Samsung Pay" -- an open list, so the clause
--          does not say whether a third-party proxy card is in or out.
--   L4     A hand-written `AND NOT (credential EQUALS Via Amaze)` conjunct,
--          plus a branch of its own returning the cl.8 rate at Unconfirmed
--          status. The gap is DECIDED here, and marked as decided.
--   BOUGHT A construct that distinguishes "this rule does not reach the case"
--          from "this rule excludes the case". Today both are spelled `NOT`.
--
-- SITE 8 -- the FAQ against the T&C, which is a conflict between SOURCES.
--   TEXT   The held FAQ, Q2: "With effect from 15 July 2024, contactless
--          payment transactions will earn 1 HSBC Reward point." The held T&C,
--          cl.5 with cl.7: a contactless tap is an Eligible Transaction at 10
--          or 20 points. They cannot both be right.
--   L4     The T&C is encoded, the FAQ is not encoded at all, and the conflict
--          is carried as a string in `conditions` on every contactless answer.
--   BOUGHT A priority relation between DOCUMENTS rather than between clauses
--          -- "the later dated instrument defeats the earlier" stated once,
--          instead of the encoder resolving it silently and leaving a note.
--          This is the one site on the list that is not about this document's
--          internal structure, and it is the one the corpus exists to find.
-- ===========================================================================
